<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>Saint Augustine's story</h4>
<p>It is important to narrate Augustine's conversion not as a distant tale, but as something that connects with everyone's experience. Augustine lived sin in his own flesh: the theft of the pears (he didn't need them; he stole for the pleasure of doing evil), his addictions, his search for love in the wrong places. And then grace found him. That is exactly what Romans 5:20 affirms: where sin increased, grace abounded all the more.</p>

<h4>The image of God</h4>
<p>Do not reduce &laquo;image of God&raquo; to intelligence or will alone. The Catechism emphasizes the capacity to <em>know and love</em> (CCC 356). We are God's image because we can enter into a relationship of love. This means the image of God is manifested especially in communion: male and female together, in community, not in isolation.</p>

<h4>Original sin</h4>
<p>A common mistake is to think that original sin was &laquo;eating a piece of fruit.&raquo; The fruit is symbolic; what is essential is the decision to want to be like God <em>without God</em>. It is the root of all sin: pride, distrust in God's love, the desire for absolute autonomy. Help the group see that the serpent does not force Eve; it tempts her. Temptation always works this way: it distorts God's word and sows doubt about his intentions.</p>

<h4>Concupiscence vs. sin</h4>
<p>It is crucial to distinguish between original sin (a historical event) and concupiscence (the inward tendency to sin that remains as a consequence). Concupiscence is not sin in itself; it is the inclination. Saint Paul describes it perfectly in Rom 7:15: &laquo;I do not do what I want, but I do the very thing I hate.&raquo; We all experience that tension.</p>

<h4>The Protoevangelium</h4>
<p>Gn 3:15 is one of the most important verses in all of Scripture. It is the first promise of salvation, given <em>immediately after</em> the Fall. God does not wait; even in punishment there is hope. The woman whose offspring will crush the serpent's head is Mary, and her offspring is Christ. Connect this with the Annunciation (Lk 1:26-38): Eve said &laquo;no&raquo; in the garden; Mary said &laquo;yes&raquo; in Nazareth.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the broken mirror metaphor: we are like a mirror that reflects God's image, but it broke. The pieces still reflect the light, but the image is distorted. Jesus comes to repair the mirror. Activities: draw the garden of Eden before and after sin, memorize Rom 5:20 with hand signs, write three things they can do out of love.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their experience of temptation: &laquo;What &lsquo;serpents&rsquo; whisper to you today that God doesn't love you?&raquo; Social media, peer pressure, the culture of &laquo;you decide what's right for you.&raquo; The story of the Fall is not a distant myth; it repeats every time we choose our own will over God's love. Compare Gn 3 with the Annunciation: Eve said &laquo;no&raquo;; Mary said &laquo;yes.&raquo;</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults carry guilt and shame from their past. Help them see that sin does not have the last word. Saint Augustine's experience is powerful: &laquo;I was made and I unmade myself.&raquo; But also: grace found him. It's not about denying evil, but about confessing that Christ has already conquered it. The key question is: &laquo;In what areas of your life do you need Christ to &lsquo;crush the serpent's head&rsquo;?&raquo;</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the theology of original sin: the distinction between personal sin and sinful condition, the sense of &laquo;sin of the world&raquo; in John, the doctrine of solidarity in Adam (monogenism vs. polygenism). Quote the Church Fathers: Irenaeus (&laquo;recapitulation&raquo;), Augustine (&laquo;mass of sin&raquo;), Anselm (satisfaction). The Adam-Christ typology (Rom 5:12-21) is central.</p>

</section>

