<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;If God is good, why did he create the serpent?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The serpent is not a creature created by God to tempt man. The Catechism teaches that the devil and the demons were angels created good by God, who freely rebelled against him (CCC 391-395). The serpent is the symbol of Satan, who uses an existing creature to tempt. God did not create evil; evil is a privation of the good, a distortion of what God made good.</p>

<h4>&laquo;Is it fair that we all bear Adam's sin?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Original sin is not personal guilt imputed to us. It is a condition, not an act. Saint Paul explains it with the image of solidarity: just as we are united to Adam by nature, we are invited to be united to Christ by grace (Rom 5:12-21). We are not guilty of Adam's sin, but we suffer its consequences, just as a child may suffer the consequences of a parent's decisions without being guilty of them (CCC 404).</p>

<h4>&laquo;Is the Eden account historical or symbolic?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The Catechism says that the Genesis account uses &laquo;figurative language&raquo; to describe a &laquo;primo-genital&raquo; event that really occurred (CCC 390). That is: the form of the narrative is symbolic (a talking serpent, magical trees), but the event is real: there was a first human pair who sinned freely, and that sin had consequences for all humanity. We do not need to take the account literally to believe that the Fall was real.</p>

<h4>&laquo;If we are already baptized, why do we keep sinning?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Baptism erases original sin, but it does not eliminate concupiscence (the tendency to sin). We continue to struggle against that inward tendency. Saint Paul describes it in Rom 7 and offers the solution in Rom 8: the Holy Spirit gives us the strength not to live according to the flesh. Baptism gives us grace, but the ongoing struggle is part of the Christian life. We are not alone: &laquo;I can do all things through him who strengthens me&raquo; (Phil 4:13).</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord, you who created us in your image<br />
and did not grow weary of seeking us when we were lost,<br />
give me a compassionate heart for those who listen to me.<br />
Help me to show that sin does not have the last word,<br />
that your mercy is greater than our frailty,<br />
that where sin increased, grace abounded all the more.<br />
May my words not be condemnation,<br />
but a bridge to your love.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

